<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kneiphof</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kneiphof was an island in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pregolya">Pregel</a> river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg">Königsberg</a>, now Kaliningrad, and one of the three medieval towns that formed the city. It held Königsberg Cathedral, begun in the 14th century as the cathedral of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of Samland, and the university. The town was destroyed in 1944–1945; the restored cathedral, with the tomb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/immanuel-kant">Immanuel Kant</a>, now stands in a park on the island.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The town stood on an island of about 10 hectares, bounded to the north by the Neue Pregel and to the south by the Alte Pregel, two branches of the Pregel River. Its name, known in medieval forms such as Knipaw and Knypabe, was of Old Prussian origin and referred to swampy land flushed by water. Early in the 14th century the island was called Vogtswerder in German, after a vogt of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a> who used it, but the townspeople kept the Prussian name of the 1327 charter.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Kneiphof was the youngest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg">Königsberg</a>'s three towns, beside Altstadt and Löbenicht, each with its own charter, market rights, church and walls. Grand Master Werner von Orseln granted it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelmno">Kulm</a> rights on 6 April 1327 and gave the eastern third of the island to the Bishopric of Samland for the cathedral. Bridges such as the Krämerbrücke and the Grüne Brücke joined it to its neighbours; five of them belong to the seven bridges of Königsberg problem, which Euler proved to have no solution. As a Hanseatic town it joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confederation-of-cologne">Confederation of Cologne</a> against King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valdemar-iv-of-denmark">Valdemar IV of Denmark</a> in 1367.</p><p>Kneiphof was a founding member of the Prussian Confederation in 1440 and rose against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a> in the Thirteen Years' War, accepting King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-iv-jagiellon">Casimir IV Jagiellon</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. When Altstadt and Löbenicht returned to the Order in 1455, Kneiphof held out behind its water and walls, helped by ships from Danzig, until hunger forced its surrender to Komtur Heinrich Reuß von <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plauen">Plauen</a> on 14 July. By the decree of 13 June 1724 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-william-i-of-prussia">Frederick William I of Prussia</a> merged the three towns into the united city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg">Königsberg</a>, and Kneiphof Town Hall served the whole city until 1927.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Devastated by the bombing of 1944, the quarter passed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg">Königsberg</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> in 1945, and material from its buildings went to rebuild cities such as Leningrad. From the 1970s the island was turned into a park with many sculptures, and the cathedral was rebuilt from the 1990s. Now called Kant Island after the philosopher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/immanuel-kant">Immanuel Kant</a>, it lies in the Moskovsky District of Kaliningrad, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kneiphof” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The war also drew in cities of the Order's eastern lands such as <b>Kneiphof</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/royal-prussia">Royal Prussia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>During the Thirteen Years' War between the Prussian Confederation, allied with Poland, and the Teutonic Order, fighting began for control of <b>Kneiphof</b>, the port district of Königsberg.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-13">13 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Battle for <b>Kneiphof</b>, fought since April for Königsberg's port district, ended in a decisive victory for the Teutonic Order.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-14">14 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The bishop received a third of the diocese, with residence at Fischhausen and a cathedral on the <b>Kneiphof</b> island in Königsberg.</blockquote><a href="/state/bishopric-of-samland">Bishopric of Samland</a></li></ul></section>
